"""Chapter introductions, takeaways, questions, and case captions."""
GUIDES={}
def add(n,raw):
    GUIDES[n]=[tuple(part.strip().replace(' ¶ ','\n\n') for part in line.split(' || ')) for line in raw.strip().splitlines() if line.strip()]
    assert all(len(row)==3 for row in GUIDES[n]),n

add(2, '''A team asks us to build a social feed and gives us one number: ten million daily users. It is tempting to start counting servers. But a person who reads a short text post creates different work from someone who uploads a photograph or publishes to a million followers. ¶ Before estimating capacity, we need to find out what a successful visit looks like and what the system must do to make it happen. In this chapter, we will turn user journeys into observable requirements, identify the requests and writes they cause, and decide what we mean when we call a system read-heavy or write-heavy. || Follow a user journey to its required outcome, then count the requests and storage operations at the boundary you are designing. || A new feature lets users upload and view images. Which additional requests, stored bytes, and transfer would you add to the workload, and which assumptions would you ask the team to supply?
Our feed is running. A dashboard reports that it completes 180 requests each second, but users say that it keeps getting slower. The completion count alone cannot tell us how many requests are arriving, how long they wait, or how many users give up. ¶ We need measurements that describe what people receive. In this chapter, we will distinguish rate from latency, examine the distribution behind an average, and separate promises about service, stored data, and observations of state. We will then turn one of these promises into a measurable objective and calculate how many failures its error budget allows. || Measure arrivals, useful completions, waits, and failures together. State the population, time window, and precise promise behind each target. || The service reports a 19.9 ms mean, but one of 100 users waited a second. Why might its p99 still be 10 ms, and what evidence should accompany that percentile?
We now know which actions the feed must support and how we will judge their results. The next question is how much work those actions produce. Ten million daily users is only a starting point: we still need assumptions about requests, posts, image views, growth, and the times when people arrive together. ¶ In this chapter, we will build a worksheet with those assumptions visible. We will calculate requests per second, daily storage growth, and image transfer separately, then consider peaks and the time needed to add capacity. The resulting estimates will tell us what to test and which resources need separate investigation. || Keep workload assumptions and units visible. Estimate each resource separately, allow for growth and peaks, and test the capacity needed to serve that work. || If users view twice as many images but publish the same number of posts, which storage and transfer estimates change? What else must you know before changing the API server count?
Suppose the estimated traffic reaches our service, every worker is busy, and the queue keeps growing. Adding workers may help, but some work cannot be divided and some requests must wait for many other calls. We need to understand these limits before choosing a larger deployment. ¶ In this chapter, we will relate average work in progress to time spent in the service, calculate the limit of parallel speedup, and see how dependent calls affect slow requests. We will then compare designs against mandatory requirements and use an explicit decision matrix to discuss the feasible choices. || Use queueing and parallelism models within their assumptions. Reject designs that fail mandatory requirements before scoring preferences, and record the evidence behind the choice. || Workers share one database that is already limiting completions. What could happen to the queue and user latency if you add workers without changing the database capacity?''')

add(3, '''Our to-do application now has a web page. A user enters its address and asks to see a task list, but the browser and application are on different machines. The request must find a destination and carry data there before the task service can do its work. ¶ We will follow this one request to connect addresses, ports, sockets, and transport protocols to concrete steps. By the end, we should be able to explain how data reaches an application and why receiving bytes is different from receiving a complete application message. || Follow the request from the browser to an addressed transport endpoint. Then check what the chosen transport delivers: application messages need framing even when TCP reliably orders their bytes. || A remote client cannot reach the task server even though it listens on port 443. Which address, route, firewall, and return-path checks would you make, and what permission check remains after communication works?
The first secure request is slower than the next, even though the server performs the same task-list query. Looking only at the application code leaves the difference unexplained: the first request may also need a name lookup, a connection, and secure setup. ¶ We will follow those stages in order, examine what TLS protects, and see how HTTP versions and connection reuse change the communication path. Then we will account for the time in a simplified cold and warm request, keeping its assumptions separate from a real measurement. || Account for lookup, connection setup, secure setup, and the request itself. A usable warm connection removes only the work that has already been completed; TLS identity checks and application permissions serve different purposes. || In the assumed 155 ms cold example, a later request uses the same usable secure connection. Which stages disappear, why does 55 ms remain, and what would you check before expecting that result in a measurement?
A browser's request may reach a proxy, a gateway, and a load balancer before it reaches the task application. These names alone do not tell us which component checks a credential, chooses a backend, or reads the HTTP path. ¶ We will trace those decisions and identify the information and trust each requires. We will then add live task updates and compare repeated polling with SSE and WebSockets. The aim is to explain both the work each arrangement saves and the connection and recovery work it adds. || Give each intermediary an explicit routing, policy, and trust responsibility. Polling and streaming change how updates travel, while missed-update recovery still needs an application rule. || A gateway validates a caller's credential and an L7 proxy routes /tasks to the task service. Where should ownership of task 42 be checked, and which component must be able to read the HTTP path?
The server saves a task, but the client receives no reply. The service may still be running and the task may still be present. The missing response leaves the caller with an outcome it cannot yet determine. ¶ We will follow traffic through network boundaries, changing service endpoints, and edge caches, then examine timeouts and borrowed connections. These mechanisms affect whether a request reaches a usable destination and how long it can wait. By the end, we should be able to describe a bounded recovery path without assuming that a timeout erased remote work. || Discovery and reachability provide possible communication paths. Bound connection use and waiting, and recover uncertain writes through a supported identity or status contract. || A create request times out and the client reconnects to a different instance. What server-side agreement would let it recover the earlier result without accidentally creating a second task?''')

add(4, '''Our service must process 1,000 file uploads. Some spend most of their time waiting for storage; others spend it compressing bytes. Before choosing how many operations to start, we need to understand where the work runs and which resources it keeps. ¶ A function can return while its background task still holds a large buffer. Several threads can share that buffer, and all can stop when their process crashes. The source files alone do not reveal these lifetimes or failure boundaries. ¶ In this chapter, we will distinguish programs, processes, and threads, follow memory and other resources through a request, and compare waiting with computation. These observations will help us choose an execution model suited to the work. || Match concurrency to the limiting resource. Follow buffers, files, and connections until their actual owner releases them, even if the initiating function has already returned. || Uploads change from mostly storage waiting to mostly compression. Which resource is now likely to limit progress, and why might more concurrent tasks fail to improve completion time?
One upload waits for a network read while another is ready to continue. An event loop can use that waiting interval to run the second task. Now suppose a callback occupies the loop with two seconds of computation: even a completed read cannot make the first task run until the loop becomes available. ¶ To explain the difference, we need to separate an unfinished operation from a blocked thread, and a ready task from an executing one. We also need to limit the jobs waiting for workers, because an execution limit alone does not bound the waiting queue. ¶ In this chapter, we will trace nonblocking I/O, concurrency, parallelism, and future completion. We will then use worker and queue limits to control how much work the service keeps in progress. || A task can be unfinished, ready, or executing. Overlapping task lifetimes provide concurrency; simultaneous execution provides parallelism. Bound waiting jobs as well as active workers. || A network read finishes while a two-second callback occupies the only event-loop thread. What has become ready, and what must happen before its task can execute?
Two upload handlers each increment a shared counter. Both calls finish, but the counter moves from zero to one. Each assignment may look reasonable on its own; the problem appears when we place both callers' reads and writes on the same timeline. ¶ A repair must protect the complete update. It also needs a coordination rule that all callers share. Once several resources need protection, their acquisition order can determine whether anyone makes progress. ¶ In this chapter, we will compare shared memory with message passing, trace a lost update, and choose mutexes, semaphores, atomics, and condition variables for their particular jobs. We will also examine deadlock, starvation, and livelock. || Protect the complete invariant with a coordination rule shared by every relevant caller. Check both the safety of updates and the ability of participants to make progress. || A and B each protect their counter update with a different private mutex. Can both still read zero, and which shared critical section would prevent the lost update?
A request ends while its child task still holds an upload buffer and a connection. Meanwhile, another thread is about to read a newly constructed task record, and new uploads continue arriving faster than workers complete them. Correct individual updates are only part of the problem. ¶ We must explain when a reader may use published state, who owns unfinished work, and how much waiting the service will permit. Cancellation of a request also needs a clear meaning: does it end the work itself, or only the connection through which it was requested? ¶ In this chapter, we will connect visibility and immutability with resource lifetimes, backpressure, and structured concurrency. We will distinguish request-owned cleanup from work whose durable owner must continue after the request ends. || Safe concurrent use requires visible initialised state, clear ownership, and bounded outstanding work. Cancellation needs local cleanup and an explicit policy for accepted work and uncertain remote effects. || A caller disconnects after an upload has been accepted for later processing. Which request resources should be released, and which state must remain under a durable owner’s responsibility?''')

add(5, '''One payment class reads an HTTP request, checks an order, calculates fees, creates a provider client, and stores the result. A fee change and a provider change both send us into the same class. Dividing it into files will help only if we also decide which rules and details each part owns. ¶ We will use this payment to distinguish abstraction, encapsulation, polymorphism, and inheritance, then compare inheritance with composition. We will give the gateway a behavioural contract and follow a likely change through the design. By the end, we should be able to explain how each boundary helps and where SOLID offers a useful question to ask. || Give payment rules and provider details clear owners. Distinguish the operation exposed, access to state, replaceable implementations, and inheritance; judge their contracts by what callers can rely on. || When a provider renames a request field but the payment rules stay the same, which part should change? Which amount, outcome, and retry meanings must its gateway contract preserve?
Our payment code now has separate collaborators. We still need to decide what to share, what callers may know about internal objects, and who should enforce a reservation rule. A shorter expression or an extra wrapper does not settle those questions. ¶ We will examine repeated business knowledge, a chain of object lookups, and an account reservation. Then we will separate operations that report information from operations that request effects. The aim is to apply each principle to a concrete dependency or rule, including the coordination needed when two callers act at once. || Share knowledge that has one meaning and owner. Limit dependence on internal representations, keep rule enforcement with its owner, and make the difference between queries and effects clear. || Two callers each try to reserve 600 from an account with 1,000 available. How does reserve(amount) help organise the rule, and what must shared storage enforce for only one reservation to succeed?
A payment keeps its identity while its status changes. Its amount and currency have value of their own, and a provider call can create an effect outside our application. Treating all three as ordinary mutable fields makes the payment difficult to reason about. ¶ We will separate calculations from effects, distinguish entities from value objects, and follow checkout's conversation with inventory and payments. We will then test the observable outcomes, including decline and uncertainty. By the end, we should be able to identify what each collaborator owns and which claims need evidence from a real storage or provider boundary. || Distinguish identity from value and calculation from effect. Follow the collaborators and their returned results, then test both required business outcomes and the real boundaries those outcomes depend on. || A test gateway returns uncertainty and the order stays pending. What has this established, and what evidence is still needed about actual provider retries and database persistence?
We want to improve the payment class without changing what its callers can rely on. Moving code can still introduce a serious error: a provider timeout might become a decline, or an amount might acquire different units. The revised class diagram would not reveal either mistake. ¶ We will make small structural changes and check the same success and failure histories after each one. We will also express payment decisions as pure functions and leave effects to a coordinator. The aim is to choose a clear representation while preserving business rules, outcome meanings, and recovery responsibilities. || Refactor in small steps while preserving observable behaviour, including uncertainty. Objects and pure functions can express the same rules; executing their requested external effects still needs explicit recovery and duplicate handling. || A refactor moves payment decisions into a pure function. Which test would catch a timeout being reclassified as decline, and why must replaying a decision remain separate from repeating a charge?''')

add(6, '''Our notification service creates an email provider inside its own code. Testing a notification rule therefore starts by dealing with provider configuration, even when the test only needs to see which message would be sent. We will move construction to startup and follow how the service receives its sender. This gives us a concrete way to distinguish dependency injection, inversion of control, and dependency inversion. || Supplying a collaborator is dependency injection. Choosing an application-owned contract can invert a source dependency. The composition root connects the concrete objects that carry out the runtime calls. || The service receives an SMS SDK through its constructor, but every method still interprets that SDK’s status codes. Which problem has injection solved, and what additional contract would keep notification policy independent of those codes?
A request creates a background job and passes it an open database session. By the time the job starts, the request has finished and closed that session. We will trace when objects are created, which callers reuse them, and who releases them. These steps explain why having a reference is different from having permission to keep using a resource. || Choose object reuse and resource ownership together. Every use must fit the resource’s valid lifetime, and cleanup must run when its owning operation succeeds, fails, or is cancelled. || A request queues a job that may run an hour later. Which identifiers should it pass, and where should the job create and close its database session?
A service receives a container and looks up whichever collaborators it needs. The constructor is short, but the service's actual needs are now scattered through its methods. We will compare this arrangement with explicit dependencies, then introduce a narrow factory for a choice that really must wait until a notification arrives. || Name known collaborators explicitly. When a runtime choice requires a factory, give it a focused operation and state whether its result is owned, shared, or borrowed. || A SenderFactory sometimes returns an application-owned email sender and sometimes creates a new SMS sender. What must its result contract tell the caller so neither sender is closed at the wrong time?
An exporter must choose a writer. A notification may need a renderer and template that agree, or several settings that must be checked together. We will follow five creational patterns through these different construction problems, including the ownership rules of copied and shared objects. || Choose a creational pattern for the construction decision it expresses: one product, a family, staged assembly, copying, or restricted sharing. Define when the result is valid and who owns it. || A notification has three required fields and two optional fields that a named-parameter constructor can validate together. What additional need would justify introducing a builder?
We add timing to a sender, translate an SMS provider's status, and simplify notification submission for a caller. All three changes can involve wrappers, yet they change different relationships. We will trace seven structural patterns by asking what each object adds, translates, shares, or controls. || Identify a structural pattern by the relationship it changes. A wrapper can translate, extend, simplify, or guard, and each purpose carries a different behavioural contract. || TimingSender(RetrySender(sender)) and RetrySender(TimingSender(sender)) both record durations. Which measures a logical call and which measures attempts, and what further contract makes the retries safe?
A service chooses a pricing rule; a payment changes from pending to captured; a receipt command waits to be executed. Each case needs a different decision to be made explicit. We will follow ten behavioural patterns through their operations and compare the changes each makes easy. || Place each algorithm, reaction, intention, transition, or coordination rule where its changes belong. The object arrangement must also state ordering, failure, and resource behaviour where these affect the operation. || A caller can choose a discount strategy, but cannot arbitrarily choose “captured” as a payment’s state. Which evidence and transition rules explain the difference?
A reservation service must load a record, check a rule, and save related changes together. A screen may need a simpler view of the same data. We will separate these responsibilities using four enterprise patterns and identify the precise database boundary at which a commit takes effect. || Repositories expose data operations, Specifications express rules, a Unit of Work owns a local commit decision, and CQRS separates command and query models. State each boundary’s actual guarantees. || A Unit of Work updates inventory and inserts a reservation in one database, then calls an external payment provider. Which changes can its local commit cover, and what happens to that claim if the provider reply is lost?''')

add(7, '''A shopping application can separate input, policy, and storage without putting them on different machines. Compare architecture styles by their dependency rules and the changes they help contain. || Code boundaries and deployment boundaries solve related but different problems. || What benefit would justify turning a well-separated module into a remote service?
Catalog and fulfilment use different meanings for a product. Model those meanings with domain experts, then choose where rules need an atomic aggregate and which facts should become events. || A model is useful within a context whose vocabulary, ownership, and invariants are understood. || Which purchase-time facts should an order retain when the current catalog changes?
An order rule should not be rewritten in controllers, broker configuration, and provider adapters. Assign the business decision, its coordination, and its technical effects to clear owners. || Preserve domain meaning while translating transport, storage, and external models at explicit boundaries. || How would an anti-corruption layer handle a provider status whose name differs from your own model?
Five services always deploy together and share writable tables. Review whether the process split delivered independence, and use evidence to evolve toward a more manageable design. || Change architecture for demonstrated ownership, load, or failure needs while protecting existing properties. || When might consolidating services make the system easier to change and recover?''')

add(8, '''A client asks to create a payment. It needs a clear resource identity, valid amount representation, possible states, and bounded ways to read results. Build the contract before selecting request syntax. || An API describes behaviour and meaning as well as message shape. || Why can creating a payment resource succeed before the provider captures money?
A collection changes between pages, and a payment reply disappears before the client receives it. Use stable continuation and operation identities to give repeated calls a defined meaning. || Compatibility preserves meanings; cursors and idempotency keys need scope, validation, and recovery rules. || What should happen when an old idempotency key is reused with a different amount?
A signed-in client can still ask for another tenant's payment or overload an export. Combine access, resource limits, deadlines, and durable progress with clear acknowledgment boundaries. || Admission, permission, and completion answer different questions and need separate enforcement. || What can a webhook receiver acknowledge safely if its effect must survive a crash?
An event saved today may be replayed a year later. Keep schemas, consumer expectations, gateway roles, and documentation usable across that lifetime. || Supported consumers include delayed and recovering readers, not only today's newest client. || Which historical samples would you retain to check that a new decoder still supports recovery?''')

add(9, '''A ledger, profile, chat history, and search index do not make the same promises. Compare their reads, changes, and recovery needs before assigning a storage family. || Choose storage from operations and invariants, then verify the selected implementation's guarantees. || What would make a document model suitable for a profile but unsuitable for an unbounded chat history?
A PDF, a checkout row, and an analytical revenue scan read different shapes of data. Separate storage interfaces and physical layouts from the logical facts they represent. || Group bytes for the access pattern while preserving the meaning of current and historical facts. || Why should changing a customer's address leave the shipping address of an old order unchanged?
An order line refers to an order and a product, but those records have different lifecycles. Model keys, cardinalities, and ownership so data stays meaningful as it changes. || Relationships need both an expressed rule and a mechanism that preserves it. || What must happen to completed order history when a product is removed from sale?
Adding a search engine also adds another recovery and deletion path. Follow raw and derived copies through retention and operation before deciding that another store is worth maintaining. || Additional engines earn their cost through a demonstrated capability and a complete lifecycle. || How could restoring an old backup bring deleted content back into a search index?''')

add(10, '''A task lookup is quick for one identifier but slow for another. Both requests reach the same database, so adding another application server does not yet tell us how to help. ¶ We need to follow the work behind the query: how records are grouped into pages, how indexes locate candidates, and when storage must supply data that memory does not hold. The planner's choices connect these structures to the SQL we write. ¶ In this chapter, we will trace a lookup and a range scan, examine the later work caused by LSM writes, and use query plans to judge an index. The aim is to identify the physical work a proposed improvement actually avoids. || Follow the query from its plan through index and data pages. Judge an index by the work it avoids and the write work it adds. || An index makes a tenant list faster, but inserting tasks becomes slower. What additional work does each insert now require?
Our application transfers money between two accounts and reports that it has saved the result. A reader may still see an earlier row version while the writer commits a new one, and a crash may occur before every changed page has reached storage. ¶ These observations raise different questions. Which changes belong together? When has the database accepted them? What information allows recovery, and which version should each reader see? ¶ In this chapter, we will separate the ACID properties, follow commit and a lost reply, and connect the recovery log to delayed page writes. We will then use a version timeline to explain how readers can have different permitted views of the same row. || Keep related writes in one transaction, wait for the promised commit result, and distinguish recovery information from the versions readers may see. || A price update commits while an older snapshot still reads the earlier price. Which visibility rule should you check before treating this as an error?
Two buyers both read that the last seat is available. Each then tries to reserve it. A transaction around each caller's work helps only if its concurrency rules prevent the conflicting decisions from both being accepted. ¶ A second example is less obvious: two doctors update different rows yet jointly leave nobody on call. Preventing one row from being overwritten does not necessarily protect a rule about several rows. ¶ In this chapter, we will write these histories in event order, compare locks with conditional updates, and distinguish read anomalies, lost updates, and write skew. We will use PostgreSQL 18 to make the isolation rules concrete and identify what must be repeated after a serialization failure. || Trace concurrent decisions in order and protect the complete invariant. A retry must repeat the reads and decisions of the failed transaction. || Two transactions each lock only the doctor they will turn off. Why can that leave the shared on-call rule unprotected?
The booking service becomes busy. Requests wait for a database connection, a seat claim must agree with its reservation record, and the backup job reports success. Each situation needs evidence about a different part of the operation. ¶ A pool limit tells us how many connections can be borrowed, but not how quickly they return. A save call tells us little about a shared transaction boundary. A completed backup job does not show that the application can use the restored data. ¶ In this chapter, we will calculate a simple pool bound, trace a multi-statement reservation transaction, and design a meaningful restore check. The conditional seat-claim example will show how an application distinguishes a successful local change from a claim that did not occur. || Keep connection ownership deliberate, give related local writes one commit boundary, and verify recovery by restoring and using the application. || A restored reservation row exists. What related data and application operation would you check before declaring the booking service recovered?''')

add(11, '''A product is read repeatedly between edits. Work out which cost a cache can avoid, how much active data fits, and which layer should hold the reusable representation. || Cache value comes from useful reuse under a valid freshness and access policy. || Why can a high hit ratio coexist with slow or incorrect user responses?
A delayed reader inserts yesterday's price after a writer invalidates it. Follow cache population, expiry, versioning, and eviction as separate decisions. || Residence time and eviction policy do not by themselves establish current source data. || How can a one-minute TTL serve a value that is more than one minute behind its source?
A cache restarts just as a product becomes popular. Control duplicate loading, absent lookups, warming, and permitted stale results before the origin becomes overwhelmed. || Bound refresh and fallback work while keeping the accepted stale and absent meanings explicit. || What should happen if background refresh keeps failing past the allowed stale age?
A cache cluster and CDN add more copies and another failure path. Compare their operations with source correctness and recovery capacity, including whether the cache should exist at all. || Evaluate each cache by saved work, maintained semantics, and the cost of losing it. || Which workload would show that removing a cache improves the whole system?''')

add(12, '''Our URL shortener begins at 100 requests per second. We want to understand how it could grow towards 100,000 requests per second, adding components only when a demonstrated limit calls for them. The first tempting change is to run more application instances. But what happens when the next request reaches a different instance, or when all the new instances contact the same database? ¶ We will begin with the work performed by one request and decide which parts can be shared across machines. That requires knowing where sessions and accepted work live, how a destination is selected, and what makes an instance ready to receive traffic. We will then examine the delay between detecting demand and making new capacity available. ¶ By the end of the chapter, you should be able to explain when more compute helps, which conditions make an application instance replaceable, and which measurements can guide growth without overwhelming a shared dependency. || Scale the measured limiting work. Freely replacing application instances requires accessible state, compatible behaviour, suitable routing, and bounded demand on shared dependencies. || A signed-in user sends one request to A and the next to B. Which state and behavioural conditions must hold for B to serve correctly, and what changes if A fails after performing a write but before replying?
Several application instances can now serve requests, but suppose the database is approaching a measured storage or write limit. We could copy its data to another database, or divide the data between owners. These changes look similar on a diagram, yet they solve different problems and create different obligations. ¶ In this chapter, we will distinguish replication from partitioning, then follow how a key selects its owner. We will compare ordered ranges with hash-based placement and examine what must happen when that placement changes. Moving data while users continue writing will let us see why calculating a destination is only one part of distribution. ¶ The aim is to choose placement from the actual queries and invariants, and to explain how a handover preserves accepted work. We will also examine a partition receiving 70% of writes, because evenly distributed storage does not establish evenly distributed traffic. || Replication makes copies; partitioning divides ownership. Placement must support the important queries and invariants, while rebalancing must preserve updates and establish one current assignment. || A new owner is added to a hash-based layout, but one key still dominates write traffic. Why might the new owner remain mostly idle, and what must a later ownership move preserve while writes continue?
A user changes a profile, receives success, and then sees the old value on the next page. At the same time, another request waits for a database connection even though the web fleet's CPU looks comfortable. Adding more application instances could leave the stale read unchanged and increase connection pressure. ¶ To understand these outcomes, we will follow a write from the primary to a replica, distinguishing receipt from application. We will then follow a request through a database proxy and a connection pool, identifying which component owns its session state and where it waits. ¶ By the end of the chapter, you should be able to connect routing to a read's freshness requirement and distinguish a connection shortage from the work holding connections busy. That evidence will help us choose a change that affects the actual bottleneck. || Route reads according to their required progress, and trace connection acquisition and hold time before changing pool sizes or adding application instances. || Ten instances each allow twenty database connections. Ten more instances are added while requests wait on database locks. How does potential connection demand change, and which measurements would help distinguish the cause from the resulting pool wait?
A burst reaches the service while workers are already busy. The queue grows, and the team proposes both more nodes and a second region. Before accepting either proposal, we need to ask which work can finish in time, which failures must be tolerated, and who may authorise a write when regions cannot communicate. ¶ This chapter follows those questions from controlled waiting to regional ownership. We will compare a copied product description with a claim to the last seat, so the difference between read capacity and write coordination becomes concrete. We will also examine dependencies that can stop recovery even when several application replicas exist. ¶ Finally, we will compare the complete cost of designs that meet the same service requirements. The goal is to justify added capacity by useful outcomes, including behaviour during failure, rather than by the size of the topology. || Queues, redundancy, regional authority, and cost must be judged against the same completion and recovery requirements. Added topology helps when it changes the actual limit without breaking the required behaviour. || A team proposes more shards and a second region for an exclusive reservation service. Which part of the write rule still needs an authoritative decision, and how should the proposal define behaviour when the regions cannot communicate?''')

add(13, '''A user starts uploading a large PDF and loses the connection before the application reports success. Some bytes may have reached storage, some may still be missing, and a document record may already exist. Before retrying, the application needs to know which upload and which file those states belong to. ¶ In this chapter, we will separate binary content from processing metadata, follow a direct upload into storage, and resume a transfer from confirmed progress. We will then use checksums and stable file versions to decide when stored bytes are ready to become processing input. || Connect each upload intent to verified, stable binary content. Confirmed transfer progress becomes usable input only after completion checks and the metadata handoff succeed. || A client finishes uploading a PDF, but the metadata transaction fails. What state should keep the file from being processed, and how could cleanup distinguish this abandoned object from an upload still in progress?
A PDF has been accepted, but searching for a phrase from it returns nothing. The search engine needs a representation of the file's text, and building that representation is another operation that can fail or fall behind. ¶ We will begin with two short documents to see how an inverted index finds candidates. Then we will follow extraction and indexing as distinct stages. Finally, we will build a replacement index while documents continue changing, and identify the evidence needed before searches can safely switch to it. || An index is a versioned representation of source data. Its update and rebuild paths must preserve the intended content, deletions, and access rules through the serving handoff. || Generation B contains the same number of documents as generation A. Give an update-and-deletion sequence that could leave B incorrect despite that matching count. What would you check before switching searches to B?
The document service produces a report each morning. It also needs to flag certain arrivals within minutes. Both tasks use incoming data, but they require different freshness and recovery behaviour. ¶ This chapter compares bounded batch jobs with continuing streams. We will examine where transformations run, how committed source changes reach another store, and how raw datasets become analytical inputs. A late event will then help us distinguish event time from processing time and decide whether a published result can still change. || Choose batch or streaming by the required freshness and recovery behaviour. Define the source-to-destination handoff, the meaning of time, and the treatment of repeated or late input. || A 10:00 event arrives at 10:03 after its one-minute window's count has been published. If consumers require corrected event-time totals, what must the processor communicate about the old result and the late arrival?
A reviewer disputes an invoice amount after both the PDF and the extractor have changed. Rerunning the current pipeline will answer a new question: what does today's transformation produce from today's file? It may not explain the earlier result. ¶ We will identify the source evidence and transformation history needed to investigate that result. We will then distinguish structural checks from checks of meaning, replay repaired work without accepting stale attempts, and follow deletion through derived copies and recovery. These steps bring the unit's separate mechanisms together into one document-processing design. || A useful output names its source and transformation, passes checks of structure and meaning, and can be repaired within a lifecycle that also covers derived copies and restored data. || A worker reads a PDF, then the user deletes it before the worker finishes. Which completion check and deletion evidence would stop that worker—and a later replay or backup restore—from republishing its extracted text?''')

add(14, '''An order service sends a payment request and then loses contact with the provider. The order still says pending. The provider may already have charged the card. We will begin by tracing this uncertainty, then ask what failures our design covers and how to order events when different machines show different times. || A missing reply leaves a remote outcome uncertain. State the failures being covered, then choose clocks and message rules that support the ordering question you actually need to answer. || A leader misses five heartbeats and is replaced. It then resumes and sends a delayed write. Explain what the missing heartbeats established, what they did not establish, and which resource check could stop the earlier owner's write.
You publish a post in one region and immediately read your feed from another. The post is missing. Later it appears, then disappears when another replica serves the next request. Each observation raises a different question about what the service promised. This chapter develops precise consistency rules by following short histories of writes and reads. || A consistency model specifies permitted histories. Identify the operation and scope, then check completed writes, overlapping operations, dependencies, and session progress against that promise. || An author publishes P and sees it, then reads from a replica that omits P. A friend who has never seen P also reads from that replica. Which result violates a promised session guarantee, and what additional rule would make the friend's later read illegal too?
Three replicas are running, but a network fault leaves one unable to talk to the other two. A customer can still reach each side. We will examine which requests can complete, what quorum overlap tells us, and how a replacement leader prevents an earlier owner from writing when it resumes. || During lost communication, an operation may need to wait to preserve its promised result. Quorum overlap provides shared participants; version rules, durable evidence, and enforced authority determine what those participants can safely establish. || A pending write reaches only A. A read of A and C returns it; a later read of B and C returns the old value. Both reads used two of three replicas. Explain why this history can violate linearizability despite quorum overlap, and identify what the read protocol must prevent.
Two workers both believe they should own the same partition. Stopping both would avoid conflicting writes, but useful work would stop too. We will connect shared decisions to their enforcement, examine assumptions hidden in remote calls, and distinguish preventing bad outcomes from making progress under stated conditions. || Safety excludes forbidden outcomes. Liveness requires progress under stated conditions. Shared decisions, resource checks, and recovery procedures must work together to provide useful service. || A booking service never assigns a seat twice, but some accepted bookings remain pending forever. Another version rejects every request immediately. Which stated progress requirement does the first version fail, and what additional requirement is needed to rule out the second?''')

add(15, '''Our task database now has copies on three machines. The primary reports that a task is saved, then stops before one copy has caught up. Can that copy take over without losing the task? ¶ We will compare who may accept writes, what a replica's acknowledgment means, and how several replicas combine evidence. These choices explain what replication protects and what still needs a protocol. || To assess replication, trace a write from its authorised receiver through the required durable copies to the success reply. Then remove the primary and ask which replica may continue that history. The number of copies alone does not answer either question. || A follower receives a record in memory and acknowledges it, but crashes before storing it durably. What would the acknowledgment need to establish for the primary to count it as a copy that survives this process loss?
The replicas agree through log index 8. A partition separates the leader from the other two machines, which must decide whether one of them can take over. Choosing a name is only the beginning: the new leader must continue the right history. ¶ We will follow command application, elections, log replication, and commitment. Raft and Paxos show why a later decision must carry evidence from earlier decisions, including while the voter group changes. || An agreed log is useful because eligible leaders preserve its committed history and replicas apply its commands consistently. Raft and Paxos combine remembered evidence with restrictions on later decisions. Their election, commit, recovery, read, and membership rules must be followed together. || A new Raft leader finds an earlier-term entry on a majority of replicas. Why is that observation alone insufficient for its usual copy-counting commit rule, and what current-term event can commit the preceding history?
Worker A pauses while holding a lease. The lease expires, and worker B takes over. A then wakes and sends the write it had planned earlier. Both processes are alive, but only one should still have authority. ¶ We will separate detecting a possible failure, transferring permission, and checking that permission where an effect is accepted. This will help us see what locks, leases, metadata services, and fencing each contribute. || A missing heartbeat can justify suspicion, and a lease can expire, while the old process remains able to send requests. Protect the effect by checking current authority at its receiver. A fencing token helps only where that receiver enforces it atomically with the protected operation. || The coordinator has issued token 8, but the protected store has only seen token 7. What must the design establish at the store before relying on it to reject later requests carrying 7?
Two regions can keep serving local writes while disconnected, but their changes may disagree when communication returns. We need to decide whether to merge the changes, preserve a conflict, or require one authority to choose. ¶ We will build a simple convergent set, examine what its merge can guarantee, and then apply the same questions to regional replication and recovery. A merge must preserve the application's intended meaning, not merely make the copies equal. || Replicas can converge on an outcome that the application cannot accept. Choose update and merge semantics from the business rule, then state where regional writes may proceed, what success requires, and how a recovered region safely rejoins. || Two regions each allocate the last item while disconnected, then merge both decrements consistently. What does their agreement establish, and which allocation rule still needs a different design?''')

add(16, '''The order service saves a customer's order and asks a receipt worker to send an email. The worker sends it, but its acknowledgment is lost. When the same message arrives again, should the worker send another receipt? To answer, we need to distinguish the record being delivered from the effect it asks for. ¶ We will follow a message from producer to broker to consumer, comparing queues, retained logs, and publication to subscribers. Then we will use short crash histories to decide what acknowledgments and delivery guarantees actually establish. Partition order and transaction boundaries will help us state precisely which progress can be recovered and which effects still need protection. || Follow the accepted message, processing attempt, committed effect, and acknowledgment as separate events. State the failure assumptions and the boundary covered by each delivery or ordering guarantee. || A consumer commits its counter update and crashes before acknowledgment. Explain why redelivery can be safe with an atomic inbox. What further problem appears if processing also sends an email?
Our consumer can recover a message after a crash, but recovery can deliver it more than once. Another record fails every time because its format is no longer understood. Repeating all work indefinitely would waste capacity, while dropping every failure would abandon valid obligations. ¶ We will separate temporary failures from records needing repair, then make a local effect safe under repeated delivery. Finally, we will examine a different use of events: preserving accepted business changes as authoritative history. The goal is to recover unfinished work without confusing transport attempts, completed effects, and the events used to reconstruct state. || Classify failures before retrying. Preserve identity through repair, commit local duplicate recognition with its effect, and distinguish transport history from the domain history used to reconstruct state. || A repaired decoder can now read a quarantined event, but that event may already have produced a local effect before failing later. Which identity and completion evidence should replay preserve, and how could skipping it affect dependent events?
An order is saved successfully, but its event is never published because the service crashes. Later, payment succeeds and shipment fails. Both histories leave work owed to another part of checkout, even though a local database transaction has finished. ¶ We will first separate the authoritative command model from a convenient read view. Then we will record publication intent alongside the order and follow how a relay delivers it. Finally, we will coordinate several local outcomes with a saga, treating compensation as further tracked business work. These examples show what a local commit can settle and what requires continuing recovery across owners. || An outbox commits local state with the obligation to publish. A saga tracks outcomes and compensation across owners. Each mechanism settles a particular gap while later effects keep their own recovery rules. || The outbox relay publishes OrderPlaced and crashes before marking it sent. What does recovery see, why can publication repeat, and which transaction should protect a subscriber’s local update?
Checkout may wait for approval long after the original request has ended. During that wait, a scheduler can restart, a worker can lose its lease, and a provider can finish an operation whose reply never arrives. Recovery needs to know which business intention still exists and which attempt currently has authority. ¶ We will compare participant-driven coordination with an explicit coordinator, then examine how engines and durable job records preserve progress. Stable identities will connect scheduled occurrences, worker attempts, and provider outcomes. We will also budget replay alongside live traffic and reconcile uncertain results, so restarting work advances the original operation safely. || Keep a durable identity for the intention and a separate record of attempts and authority. Recovery can then reject stale completion, limit replay, and reconcile uncertain outcomes against the original operation. || Attempt seven loses its lease and attempt eight starts. Meanwhile, the provider reports that the original payment succeeded although the workflow says pending. Which checks should govern worker completion, and how should recovery use the provider result?''')

add(17, '''Two application replicas run on different processes, so we expect one to keep serving if the other stops. During an incident, both lose access to the same credential source and fail together. Later, a single surviving replica cannot carry the traffic that the pair had been serving. ¶ These histories raise different questions: which causes can remove several replicas at once, what information survives, and how much work the survivors can complete? In this chapter, we will map failure domains, separate availability from durability, and examine the state, capacity, and authority needed for active-active and active-passive operation. || Redundancy protects the stated service only when survivors have independent failure placement, usable state, sufficient capacity, and a tested path to take over. || Two instances each serve 70% of their measured capacity. After one fails, what must change for the survivor to preserve the required service?
During peak traffic, the payment provider slows down. Calls retain connections while they wait, new requests queue, and callers begin retrying. Soon the application has few resources left even for requests whose dependencies are healthy. ¶ Repeating the calls cannot restore the missing capacity. We need to decide how long work remains useful, which failures justify another attempt, and how much recovery traffic the service can afford. We will follow deadlines, retry budgets, circuit breakers, resource isolation, and rate limits to see where each can interrupt this sequence. || Deadlines bound useful lifetime; retry budgets bound repeated work; breakers, bulkheads, and rate limits protect the resources through which failures spread. || Four nested layers each allow three attempts. Why can jitter spread those attempts without solving their total load, and where should the overall limit apply?
The recommendation service is unavailable, but the catalog still works. We can show a useful product page without recommendations. If the ledger is unavailable, however, a convenient default balance cannot establish whether a payment is permitted. ¶ The challenge is to define useful reduced service while preserving the meaning of each operation. We will choose work to shed, examine graceful degradation and fallback paths, and distinguish health checks that control restarting from those that control incoming traffic. Each decision must account for the capacity and business rules that remain. || Choose reduced service by its business meaning, and check that its permissions, freshness, and capacity remain acceptable during the failure. || Why can a cached help page remain useful during an outage while a default account balance cannot justify a payment?
The replacement machines are running, yet checkout still cannot resume. A restored database is too old, an encryption key is missing, or a repaired primary attempts to write using its former authority. Infrastructure can return before the required business service does. ¶ We will set recovery time and data-loss objectives, follow a complete restore and authority handover, and test the failure scenarios that the design promises to cover. We will then use service objectives, controlled fault exercises, and incident evidence to choose changes that reduce the causes and consequences of future outages. || Recovery restores useful service, required history, and valid authority. Measure the whole journey, then use drills and incident evidence to test specific improvements. || A drill brings machines back quickly but restores only old data. Which recovery objective may still be missed, and what else must be checked before declaring recovery?''')

add(18, '''A document service serves two organisations. An administrator from tenant A signs in successfully and changes a document identifier in the request to one belonging to tenant B. The service recognises the administrator, yet must still reject the read. ¶ We will follow this request from the browser through identity validation and the resource permission check. Along the way, we will distinguish login from delegated access, connect roles to their tenant scope, and decide how much authority a worker needs. The aim is to explain which evidence permits a particular action, rather than assuming a successful login permits every later request. || Validate identity first, then decide whether that actor may perform this action on this resource within the permitted tenant. Preserve that distinction in roles, protocol tokens, and worker permissions. || A tenant A administrator has a valid session and knows tenant B document 7. Which identity, tenant, resource, and action checks should the request pass through, and where should it be denied?
A private file is encrypted, but its processing worker can read every file and obtain every decryption key. If the worker is compromised, the encryption may offer little protection against the actions that identity can already perform. ¶ We will trace the real access paths, then examine complementary controls: limited credentials, protected transport and storage, suitable password hashing, key custody, and network boundaries. We will also rotate a credential and consider restoring old encrypted data. The goal is to identify what each control protects and what must remain available for legitimate recovery. || Trace credentials, plaintext, and decryption authority through their real lifecycles. Rotation must retire old authority without accidentally destroying the keys and access needed for required recovery. || New files use key version two. Which key references, permissions, and older versions must a restore test exercise before version one can be retired?
Tenant-scoped database queries work correctly, but a cache mixes two documents numbered 7. Later, a deletion removes the database row while an old extraction job recreates its text. Both failures occur outside the original query. ¶ We will carry trusted tenant context through caches, jobs, objects, and exports, then follow private data through diagnostics, audit, retention, and deletion. The goal is to account for the copies and delayed work that can expose or recreate data after the main request has ended. || Tenant isolation follows data through every store and worker. Deletion must account for those copies, delayed publication, and restore paths, while audit records describe the outcomes actually reached. || A document is deleted while an extraction job waits. Which copies and worker transitions must the deletion process account for so that the document does not reappear?
A reviewed change becomes a running release through dependencies, builds, stored artifacts, and deployment credentials. Each step can change what reaches production. Users also need recovery paths that are practical without granting authority to the wrong organisation. ¶ We will examine the release path and the user journey, then bring the unit's controls together in a private export review. We will change membership, cancellation, and deletion state while the job waits. The aim is to state what should happen at each later boundary and identify evidence that it actually happens. || Connect the reviewed source to the deployed artifact, provide workable bounded recovery, and test sensitive journeys as authority and data state change. || Membership is revoked after an export is queued. What should happen at execution, publication, and download, including a capability issued before revocation?''')

add(19, '''A user waits five seconds for a payment request. The CPU dashboard looks ordinary, so adding processor capacity has no clear justification yet. We need to find where the time went and whether this request represents a wider problem. ¶ We will combine population measurements, individual request timelines, and structured event records. We will then follow tracing context across calls and messages and relate the user's symptom to traffic, errors, and resource pressure. The aim is to ask a precise question of each signal and connect the answers into an explanation we can test. || Use metrics to establish the population symptom, traces to locate work and waits, and logs to explain relevant events. Correlate their evidence without treating one history as the whole population. || A latency histogram shows a worsening p99 and one trace shows a long connection wait. What does each establish, and what further evidence would support applying that explanation to the wider slowdown?
The connection pool is full and request durations rise, but the on-call engineer is already receiving pages about harmless CPU fluctuations. Collecting and alerting on every available measurement has made it harder to recognise the condition that needs action. ¶ We will connect request measurements to resource measurements, define eligible outcomes and a service objective, and calculate how quickly failures consume its allowance. We will then choose alerts, sampling policies, and dashboard views for the decisions they must support. By the end, we should be able to explain both what an urgent signal means and which useful response should follow it. || Define eligible outcomes and the objective before calculating budget burn. Choose bounded telemetry, dashboards, and alerts that support a clear decision and a useful response. || A 99.9% success objective has an observed 1% bad-event fraction. How do you calculate burn rate, and what population, window, and response information would you need before turning it into a page?
A deployment precedes a slowdown. One responder proposes faster SQL, another proposes more CPU, and a third wants to restart the service. The shared timing is a clue, but it does not show which part of the request became slower or which intervention would help. ¶ We will distinguish execution from waiting, use profiles and traces to examine specific mechanisms, and compare the affected paths with normal ones. We will also coordinate incident actions and write procedures that connect observations, supported mitigations, and recovery checks. The goal is to restore useful service while preserving the evidence needed to explain the incident. || Locate the extra wait or execution, then test the mechanism that could cause it. Coordinate mitigation, preserve state and evidence, and verify useful service as well as cleared alerts. || The five-second path contains 4.6 seconds of connection acquisition and 100 ms of query execution. Which observations would help explain the acquisition wait, and how should responders coordinate a mitigation and its recovery check?
The HTTP service has recovered, yet some accepted extraction jobs remain unfinished. Meanwhile, the team's incident notes say only “bad release,” and a fleet average hides a shard approaching its storage limit. Each view leaves out part of the system's continuing work. ¶ We will turn incident evidence into specific improvements, monitor capacity at the owner that can limit progress, and follow asynchronous operations beyond the original request. We will distinguish the age of unfinished work from the duration of completed work. The aim is to recognise recovery and ongoing harm at the business outcome the system promised. || Use incidents to improve the conditions that shaped them. Monitor limiting owners and follow durable operation identities until the required outcome, including work still unfinished after serving recovers. || Acceptance remains healthy while document completions stop. Which operation ages, stage timings, and durable records would you inspect, and why can completed-job durations alone hide the stuck work?''')

add(20, '''The same image works locally and fails in production. Compare environments and runtime isolation, keeping persistent state, resources, and platform duties explicit. || Packaging improves repeatability only with controlled configuration and appropriate state ownership. || Which data would disappear if a container's writable layer were replaced?
The scheduler cannot place new work, but existing requests still succeed. Separate control and serving paths, then examine startup, scaling, configuration, and replacement dependencies. || Running traffic and creating replacement capacity can depend on different paths. || Which dependency could let current instances serve while preventing every replacement from starting?
A release writes a new field, then a rollback restores a reader that cannot understand it. Keep artifacts, flags, configuration, and migration stages compatible while versions overlap. || Release safety includes data and protocol transitions, not only selecting an old executable. || At what point in expand-and-contract does rollback to the old reader become unsafe?
A managed platform offers useful capabilities while leaving application choices in your hands. Compare delegated work, limits, topologies, and internal developer needs under the same service requirements. || A platform abstraction should simplify repeated work while exposing the limits its users must manage. || Which correctness and recovery responsibilities remain with a team using a managed database?''')

add(21, '''A service misses its response-time goal. Budget the path, locate the limit, and design a benchmark that can test the proposed explanation without hiding waiting or errors. || Performance evidence is meaningful only with its workload, conditions, and outcome definition. || How could a load generator accidentally omit the very delays users would experience?
Extra nodes, caches, and transfer can change both capacity and cost. Compare complete architectures using explicit inputs and useful outcomes rather than one attractive infrastructure rate. || Filter by required behaviour before ranking cost, and keep modelled quantities distinct from measured results. || Why can a lower cost per attempt coexist with a higher cost per completed order?
An optimisation saves CPU while adding transfer, or a test suite adds checks without covering failures. Evaluate the whole effect and choose evidence for the particular risk. || Efficiency and validation should reduce real work or uncertainty without weakening the required contract. || Which failure window would a process kill before work begins leave untested?
A migration has matching row counts but wrong tenant ownership. Validate identities and invariants, preserve important regressions, and return to the requirements that justified the design. || Pair each requirement with evidence and keep unresolved targets clearly labelled. || What additional evidence would show that an equal-count migration preserved the right data?''')

CASE_OPENINGS={1: 'A visitor opens a short link and expects to reach the right page. Behind this small action, the service '
    'must find the original URL and decide whether its owner still permits the redirect.\n'
    '\n'
    'We will follow one link from creation to use and revocation. This will help us decide which records '
    'need an authoritative owner, which reads may use a cache, and which work can wait until after the '
    'visitor receives a reply.',
 2: 'Suppose three application instances each start with ten tokens for the same customer. Each instance '
    'follows its local arithmetic correctly, yet together they may admit thirty requests at once.\n'
    '\n'
    'Before building a rate limiter, we must decide what the allowance covers. We will begin with one '
    'bucket, trace its refill and spending, and then examine what changes when several processes or regions '
    'share the policy.',
 3: "A chat message appears on the sender's screen. The recipient's phone has just disconnected. What should "
    '“sent” mean while that phone is unreachable?\n'
    '\n'
    'We will store the conversation before relying on live delivery, then let a returning device recover '
    'what it missed. Following those two paths will help us distinguish saved history, device delivery, '
    'human reading, and approximate presence.',
 4: 'A password-reset message reaches a user hours after it was requested. The sender may have completed its '
    'work, yet the notification has missed its purpose.\n'
    '\n'
    'We will follow a durable notification intent through channel selection, provider submission, and later '
    'evidence. The design must keep accepted work recoverable while recognising that provider acceptance, '
    'device delivery, and human viewing are different outcomes.',
 5: 'A popular author publishes a post. Preparing a copy for every follower can make later reads quick, but '
    'many followers may never open their feeds. Waiting until each read avoids that unused work and makes '
    'the read more expensive.\n'
    '\n'
    'We will compare both journeys and build a hybrid where it helps. Throughout, finding and ranking '
    "candidates must preserve the viewer's permission to see the underlying posts.",
 6: 'An encoder has written two of the three segments required for a video. If the service now displays '
    '“Ready,” a viewer may start playback and encounter a missing piece.\n'
    '\n'
    'We will follow uploaded bytes through validation, conversion, and publication. A playable generation '
    "must name a verified set of outputs, and the delivery path must enforce the video's access and deletion "
    'rules.',
 7: 'Two riders request a trip, and both matchers choose the same nearby driver. The search may be accurate, '
    'yet it has not decided who receives the assignment.\n'
    '\n'
    'We will separate recent-location search from the authoritative claim on a driver. That distinction lets '
    'us improve matching and distribute location traffic while preserving one valid active assignment.',
 8: 'A buyer holds a seat and starts payment. The hold expires, another buyer reserves the seat, and the '
    'first payment finally succeeds. A paid flag alone cannot tell us which ticket may safely be issued.\n'
    '\n'
    'We will trace seat ownership, versioned holds, and provider evidence through that sequence. The same '
    'rules must also handle repeated requests and delayed expiry tasks without selling one seat twice.',
 9: 'A provider charges successfully, but the payment service stops before recording the reply. The client '
    'sees a timeout and tries again. How can the service recover the existing charge instead of requesting '
    'another logical payment?\n'
    '\n'
    'We will give the operation a durable identity, preserve uncertain outcomes, and reconcile provider '
    'evidence. We will also connect that evidence to balanced local postings, because a provider result, an '
    'order status, and an accounting record describe different parts of the journey.',
 10: 'A worker pauses while running a scheduled job. Its lease expires, so a replacement begins. When the '
     'first worker resumes, both may be capable of doing work even though only one currently owns the '
     'attempt.\n'
     '\n'
     'We will give the schedule, occurrence, attempt, and effect separate identities. This lets recovery '
     'recognise what it is resuming and shows why rejecting an old local completion is only one part of '
     'protecting an external effect.',
 11: 'Two collaborators insert text at the same place. One replica receives X and then Y; the other receives '
     'Y and then X. If each simply applies arrival order, their documents may differ.\n'
     '\n'
     'We will define what an edit means and how its identity and context survive repeated delivery and '
     'reconnects. We will then examine the separate questions of durable acceptance, safe history cleanup, '
     'and permission to keep editing.',
 12: 'A document has finished extraction, but some fields are uncertain and need review. Meanwhile, its '
     "owner requests deletion. A worker's finished flag cannot explain what may now be published.\n"
     '\n'
     'We will trace immutable input through extraction, validation, human decisions, and publication. The '
     'workflow must keep those stages recoverable, preserve the source of every result, and reject stale '
     'work after its authority or document has been withdrawn.'}

CASE_CAPTIONS={1: ['A link has one owner and a unique code. The stored operation identifies its creation result.',
     'Creation and redirect use authoritative mappings; analytics is a separate obligation.',
     'Test a collision, a lost reply, and a stale cached target at their different boundaries.',
     'Add capacity for a measured limit while preserving code uniqueness and revocation.'],
 2: ['A bucket groups scope, policy, tokens, and refill time in one coordinated record.',
     'Derive trusted scope before making the refill-and-spend decision.',
     'Two callers must not independently spend the same last token.',
     'Move from local to shared admission only with an explicit scope and allocation rule.'],
 3: ['History, membership, and receipt progress have different owners and lifetimes.',
     'Store the message before reporting persistence, then deliver and recover by identity.',
     'A missed live frame is recovered from history; repeating a send retains its original identity.',
     'Scale connections and fan-out without losing per-conversation progress.'],
 4: ['Intent, delivery tasks, attempts, and callbacks record different stages of the notification.',
     'Durable intent reaches a bounded channel worker and a provider with explicit outcome meanings.',
     'A sent message with a lost reply is uncertain, so recovery needs provider evidence or a stated '
     'duplicate policy.',
     'Add channels and routes after defining their expiry, priority, and retry behaviour.'],
 5: ['The post and access relationships are authoritative; feed candidates are derived references.',
     'Combine candidates, enforce visibility, rank, and return a bounded page.',
     'Repeated fan-out and stale candidates must not create duplicate or forbidden visible posts.',
     'Prepare common feeds only where measured reading saves more work than publication adds.'],
 6: ['A published manifest generation names identified, verified outputs from a particular input and '
     'profile.',
     'Uploaded bytes become playable only after required transformation and publication checks.',
     'Partial segment output stays unpublished until the required generation is complete.',
     'Scale encoding separately from delivery and preserve versioned playable sets.'],
 7: ['Latest position helps search; driver and trip state decide exclusive assignment.',
     'Candidate search precedes a valid conditional offer acceptance.',
     'Two riders can choose one candidate, but only one authoritative claim may win.',
     'Partition location work without creating conflicting assignment owners.'],
 8: ['Seat, hold, payment, and ticket records must preserve their defined ownership relationships.',
     'A hold and payment workflow lead to a version-checked confirmation or supported recovery.',
     'An old expiry or late payment must not overwrite a newer seat owner.',
     'Add admission and partition independent events while protecting the same seat invariant.'],
 9: ['Operation identity links provider evidence to payment state and balanced accounting entries.',
     'Persist intent, obtain or reconcile provider evidence, and commit the required local result.',
     'A lost provider reply leaves uncertainty rather than proof that no charge occurred.',
     'Scale useful independent work while keeping each prior operation and posting explainable.'],
 10: ['One scheduled occurrence can have several attempts, while retaining one logical run identity.',
      'Create due work durably before a current worker attempts its effect.',
      'A replaced worker can resume, so its stale completion must be rejected at the protected boundary.',
      'Separate scan capacity from execution while preserving occurrence and effect identities.'],
 11: ['Operations, snapshots, and retained context describe the same document history under one protocol.',
      'Local edits become accepted operations and reconnectable history through explicit sync rules.',
      'Concurrent or repeated operations need deterministic interpretation and stable identity.',
      'Distribute documents and compact history only within the supported offline and merge contract.'],
 12: ['Fields and decisions retain tenant, input, transform, and evidence relationships.',
      'Ingestion, extraction, review, and publication have distinct durable progress.',
      'A stale attempt cannot publish after its authority or document has been withdrawn.',
      'Scale stages independently while retaining provenance, access, and deletion behaviour.']}
